[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit"]
[Variation "Slav Defense: Three Knights Variation, 4...Bf5"]
[ECO "D15"]
[Character "positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "pawn-grab"]
[Themes "hit-b7,queen-trade"]
[For "white"]
[Kind "main"]
[Opponent "4...Bf5"]
[Start "slav 4.Nc3"]

4... Bf5 { Brings the bishop out before ...e6 shuts it in. Now nothing guards b7. } ( 4... g6 5. e3 Bg7 6. Bd3 O-O 7. O-O { Black's bishop on g7 bites on granite: the pawn on d4 is well guarded. White develops and aims for e4 later. } ) 5. cxd5 { Swaps on d5 first, so ...dxc4 is no longer possible and the queen can go to b3. } 5... cxd5 ( 5... Nxd5 6. Qb3 { Hits b7 and the knight on d5 at the same time. } ) 6. Qb3 { With the bishop on f5, nothing guards b7, and the queen also eyes d5. } 6... Qb6 ( 6... Nc6 7. Qxb7 Bd7 8. Qb3 { Takes the pawn and comes straight back before Black's rook reaches b8. White is a pawn up. } ) ( 6... Bc8 7. Bf4 { The bishop had to go home, so White is two moves ahead in development. } ) 7. Qxb6 axb6 8. e3 { Opens the bishop on f1. Black's pawns on b7 and b6 are doubled, and c7 is a weak square. } 8... e6 9. Bb5+ { Check. If the king moves, Black can no longer castle; if a piece blocks, it is pinned. Plan: Ne5 and Bd2, then Rc1 and Nb5 against c7 and the doubled b-pawns. } *
